---
import Main from "@/layouts/Main.astro";
import { siteSettings } from "@/config/siteSettings";

const siteName = siteSettings.branding.siteName;
---

<Main title={`About | ${siteName}`}>
  <section class="prose" data-bible-autolink>
    <h1>About {siteName}</h1>

    <aside class="card-panel" style="padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;">
      <p><strong>If you are in danger right now:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          If you are thinking about ending your life, call or text <strong>988</strong> (the Suicide and
          Crisis Lifeline in the United States), or call <strong>911</strong>. Tell someone near you
          today.
        </li>
        <li>
          If you or a child are being harmed, get to a safe place and call <strong>911</strong>.
          Abuse is a crime as well as a sin, and it should be reported to the authorities.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Then reach out to your pastor. You do not have to carry this alone.</p>
    </aside>

    <blockquote>
      <p>&ldquo;Your testimonies are indeed my delight; they are my counselors.&rdquo;</p>
      <p><cite>&mdash; Psalm 119:24</cite></p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      {siteName} answers common questions about biblical counseling: what it is, how the heart changes,
      how God meets us in suffering, and how the Bible speaks to struggles such as anxiety, anger, grief,
      addiction, conflict and shame.
    </p>

    <p>
      Biblical counseling rests on a simple conviction: God&apos;s Word is sufficient for the
      problems of the soul. &ldquo;All Scripture is God-breathed and is useful for instruction, for
      conviction, for correction, and for training in righteousness, so that the man of God may be
      complete, fully equipped for every good work&rdquo; (2 Timothy 3:16-17). Christ&apos;s
      &ldquo;divine power has given us everything we need for life and godliness&rdquo; (2 Peter
      1:3).
    </p>

    <h2>How to use this site</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Each answer is short, with the Scripture that grounds it listed underneath.</li>
      <li>
        Most questions have a fuller explanation under &ldquo;Read the full explanation,&rdquo;
        often with practical steps.
      </li>
      <li>Hover over or tap any Scripture reference to read the verse (Berean Standard Bible).</li>
      <li>Browse by category or search for what you are facing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What this site is not</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Not a substitute for your church.</strong> Counseling belongs in the life of a local church,
        where believers who know you can &ldquo;instruct one another&rdquo; (Romans 15:14). Take what
        you read here to your pastor or a trusted, mature believer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> See a doctor about physical symptoms, and do not stop or
        change any medication without your doctor.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not affiliated with any organization.</strong> This site is independent. It is grateful
        for the work of the Association of Certified Biblical Counselors and others, and links to them
        on the Resources page.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where the answers come from</h2>

    <p>
      The answers were drafted with the help of AI for Michael Coughlin to review, and are marked as
      such. They draw on the historic biblical counseling movement and on writers such as Matthew
      Statler, whose
      <a href="https://suffering.catechize.ing">Catechism of Suffering</a> is a companion to the Suffering
      and Hope section. Weigh every answer against the Scripture cited under it, as the Bereans did (Acts
      17:11).
    </p>

    <h2>Freely given</h2>

    <p>
      Counseling from God&apos;s Word is ministry, and ministry should be given freely (Matthew
      10:8). Everything on this site is in the public domain. See
      <a href="https://dorean.ing">dorean.ing</a> for why counseling should be supported by the church,
      not sold by the hour.
    </p>
  </section>
</Main>
